## 5. BULLET RULES

**Formula:** `Action verb + what I built/did + technology + measurable result (+ business impact only if true)`

**Priority:** Impact > Scale > Technology > Task.

**Example (every claim is supportable):**
> Reduced app startup time from 30s to 8s (73%) by parallelizing independent API calls with Future.wait and adding cache-first loading.

**So-what test:** every bullet must show impact, scale, complexity or ownership.

**If no honest number exists,** use scope or complexity: offline support, countries served, sole owner, modular architecture, payments, CI/CD. Never guess a number. If a real number likely exists, write `[ADD: metric]` and list it in the questions.

**Strong verbs:** Built, Designed, Architected, Developed, Led, Owned, Launched, Delivered, Scaled, Reduced, Improved, Optimized, Automated, Integrated, Implemented, Migrated.
**Banned openers:** Responsible for, Worked on, Helped, Assisted, Participated in, Involved in, Tasked with.

**Quantify where true:** users, MAU, downloads, countries, latency, startup time, crash rate, ANR rate, memory, API response time, retention, conversion, test coverage, build time, release frequency.

**Show, don't list.** Skills appear inside bullets as evidence.
**Ownership:** I am the sole mobile developer. Use Owned, Led, Architected, Delivered end-to-end, only where true.
**Design depth (for MAANG):** where true, show a design decision and its trade-off (why this architecture, caching strategy, state management choice). This is what interviewers probe.
**SDE-2 evidence:** mentoring, code reviews, architecture decisions, release ownership, cross-team work. If absent, ask me. Don't invent.
**Length:** one line per bullet ideally, two max. STAR is internal, never on the page.

